Out of curiosity, why is it 'becoming the enemy?' I use Google groups
now because of the convenience factor and I don't think of it as a bad
newsreader.

Which is to say, I don't think it's a particularly *good* newsreader,
any more than I think Hotmail is a particularly good free email
service, but it does the job, if imperfectly. I can follow the threads
with ease, On logging in, Google shows me new activity in threads to
which I've posted, and it also brings up a list of activity in old
threads. If I miss a couple days, I will likely have to page back to
look at the posts I missed, but this doesn't strike me as any more
tedious than the 'olden days' when I used PINE to browse usenet.

Quoting is painless. It takes two magic mouse clicks instead of one.

And it isn't attractive, no, but imho it's not meant to be pretty.
Since we are here discussing a game whose default interface is
virtually incomprehensible to a neophyte, I'm surprised Raisse even
brought this up. ;)

Certainly it has drawbacks. I may miss posts here and there if I don't
monitor the group with regularity. I don't see the [S] significator for
Slash'Em posts, but for the most part the contents of the post give it
away. Since it's proper netiquette to restate the information in the
topic anyway, I'm not certain it matters that much.

What else does Google Groups do that's evil? Please enlighten a poor
bard, if you would be so kind.



